title: Revert
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: overview
aliases: revert
summary: A revert undoes a past label or operation in place — writing back the state it replaced as a new write — while everything after it stays.

A ''revert'' answers "that one rename was a mistake", even in a watched repository, where
dozens of unrelated watcher writes may have landed since. It undoes a label, or chosen
operations: the daemon computes the state that change replaced and writes it back as an
ordinary new write. The log grows by operations saying so, and nothing leaves it — the
mistake and its correction are both on the record, which is what an audit log is for.

A revert is an ordinary write in every other respect: it can itself be reverted
(restoring the original change), pruned and trimmed. What marks it is `reverts_op_id` on
each of its operations, naming what it undid.

A revert goes back to what the change replaced, whatever was written since on the same
field: a later write is overwritten, and the plan says so first ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). Undo and redo are reverts, of a label they choose for you
([[Undo and redo]]).

